The following test case is failing because our identity hash func creates a recursive cycle, and we're not able to distinguish between the cycle caused by `foo(1)` and the one caused by `foo(2)`.

```
    def test_recursive_hash_func(self):
        def hash_int(x):
            return x

        @st.cache(hash_funcs={int: hash_int})
        def foo(x):
            return x

        self.assertNotEqual(foo(2), foo(1))
```

### Summary

Currently, when using st.multselect, we have to enter it one by one. If we can simply copy multiple options into the st.multiselect, e.g. "option1, option2, option3, ...", then it selects all those options (can choose to separate by space or comma), it would be much easier to use. 

### How?

Just allow st.multiselect to accept string input, then use delimitator to seperate options that need to be selected. 

### Solution
In `streamlit/frontend/lib/src/components/widgets/Multiselect/Multiselect.tsx`, we can use the `onInputChange`
Here is the example to do so with example from `baseui`

```
import React, {useRef} from 'react';
import { Select, TYPE, Value } from 'baseui/select';

export default function Example() {
  const [value, setValue] = React.useState<Value>([]);
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  const controlRef = useRef(null);
  const options = [
    { id: 'AliceBlue', color: '#F0F8FF' },
    { id: 'AntiqueWhite', color: '#FAEBD7' },
    { id: 'Aqua', color: '#00FFFF' },
    { id: 'Aquamarine', color: '#7FFFD4' },
    { id: 'Azure', color: '#F0FFFF' },
    { id: 'Beige', color: '#F5F5DC' },
    { id: 'tesadfasdfaft', color: '#F5FCCC' },
  ];
  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    const inputEvent = event.nativeEvent;
    if (inputEvent.inputType == "insertFromPaste") {
      const inputTextValue = inputEvent.target.value;
      const items = inputTextValue.split(",").map(item => item.trim())
      const newValues = options.filter(option => items.includes(option.id))
      setValue([...value, ...newValues.filter(item => !value.includes(item))])
      if (newValues.length > 0) {
        controlRef.current.setInputValue("")
      }
    }
  };
  return ( <Select
    controlRef={controlRef}
    inputRef={inputRef}
    options={options}
    labelKey="id"
    valueKey="color"
    placeholder="Choose a color"
    maxDropdownHeight="300px"
    type={TYPE.search}
    multi
    onChange={(event) => {
      setValue(event.value);
    }}
    onInputChange = {handleInputChange}
    value={value}
  />);
}
```

If now we copy 'AliceBlue, Aqua', then those two will be selected. 
Hopefully we can adopt this for the streamlit.

